rrot":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/parrot","n":"Parrot","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Parrots (order Psittaciformes), also known as psittacines () from the name of the type genus Psittacus, are birds with a strong curved beak, upright stance, and clawed feet. They are classified in four families that contain roughly 410 species in 101 genera, found mostly in tropical and subtropical regions. The four families are the Psittaculidae (Old World parrots), Psittacidae (African and New World parrots), Cacatuidae (cockatoos), and Strigopidae (New Zealand parrots). One-third of all parrot species are threatened by extinction, with a higher aggregate extinction risk (IUCN Red List Index) than any other comparable bird group. Parrots have a generally pantropical distribution with several species inhabiting temperate regions as well. The greatest diversity of parrots is in South America and Australasia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-parrot"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parrot"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine",[["Parrot pie","dish/parrot-pie"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bird meat","ingredient/bird-meat"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ad/Rainbow_lorikeet_%28Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus%29_Sydney.jpg/500px-Rainbow_lorikeet_%28Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus%29_Sydney.jpg","w":500,"h":647,"by":"Charles J. Sharp","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainbow_lorikeet_(Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus)_Sydney.jpg"}},"ingredient/pea":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pea","n":"Pea","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Pea is a pulse or fodder crop, but the word often refers to the seed or sometimes the pod of this flowering plant species. Peas are eaten as a vegetable. The pea is native to the Mediterranean region and southwest Asia, and cultivated nearly worldwide."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pea"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pea"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Bavarian cuisine","cuisine/bavarian-cuisine",[["Rumford's Soup","dish/rumford-s-soup"]]],["Métis foodways","cuisine/metis-foodways",[["Rubaboo","dish/rubaboo"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Peas, dry","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":13868572,"top":[["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",3297080],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",3173525],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",1459423],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",988400],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",734214],["France","cuisine/french-cuisine",496556],["Ukraine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",427224],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",405065],["Germany","cuisine/german-cuisine",312220],["Ashmore and Cartier Is.","cuisine/australian-cuisine",282546],["United Kingdom","cuisine/british-cuisine",198288],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",192351],["Spain","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",191566],["Lithuania","cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine",158016],["Kazakhstan","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",136721]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Pearl barley","ingredient/pearl-barley"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Sour beer","ingredient/sour-beer"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bc/Doperwt_rijserwt_peulen_Pisum_sativum.jpg/500px-Doperwt_rijserwt_peulen_Pisum_sativum.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Rasbak","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doperwt_rijserwt_peulen_Pisum_sativum.jpg"}},"ingredient/omurice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/omurice","n":"Omurice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Omurice or omu-rice is a Japanese dish consisting of fried rice topped with a special omelette. Omurice is most commonly cooked at home. It is often featured in Japan's version of a children's meal, okosama-ranchi (お子様ランチ)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-omurice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omurice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Volga rice","dish/volga-rice"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Breaded cutlet","ingredient/breaded-cutlet"],["Fried rice","ingredient/fried-rice"],["Omelette","ingredient/omelette"],["Tonkatsu","ingredient/tonkatsu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/0d/Omurice_by_Taimeiken.jpg/500px-Omurice_by_Taimeiken.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Kanesue","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omurice_by_Taimeiken.jpg"}},"dish/zapiekanka":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/zapiekanka","n":"Zapiekanka","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Polish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A zapiekanka is a toasted open-face sandwich made of a sliced baguette or other long roll of bread, topped with sautéed white mushrooms, cheese and sometimes other ingredients, such as ham. Served hot with ketchup, it has been a popular street food in Poland since the 1970s."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-zapiekanka"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zapiekanka"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Agaricus bisporus","ingredient/agaricus-bisporus"],["Baguette","ingredient/baguette"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Fungus","ing